---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import households from "../data/households.json";
---
<Base title="The households">
  <h1>The households</h1>
  <p class="lede">Seven reconstructed Falco families of the parish of Sant'Andrea at Arienzo, covering
  1759 to 1869 — read out of the parish registers entry by entry. The first three carry the direct line;
  the rest are its branches.</p>

  {households.map(h => (
    <section style="margin:2rem 0">
      <h2 style="margin-top:1rem">{h.name}</h2>
      <div class="scroll"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Event</th><th>Date</th><th>What the register says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{h.members.map(m => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{m.person}</strong><br/><span class="small">{m.role}</span></td>
            <td>{m.event}</td>
            <td class="mono">{m.date}</td>
            <td><em>{m.evidence}</em>{m.ark ? <><br/><span class="mono small">{m.ark}</span></> : null}</td>
          </tr>))}</tbody>
      </table></div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>What these tables establish</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The line reaches a generation further back than it did.</strong> Pasquale Falco's
    baptism of 24 October 1767 names his father <strong>Matteo Falco</strong> and his mother
    <strong>Francesca Crisci</strong>. Matteo replaces the unproven Agostino Falcone.</li>
    <li><strong>Vincenzo's marriage names four people at once</strong> — himself, his father Pasquale,
    his mother Chiara Rivetti, and his wife Andreana's father Arcangelo Crisci. It is the single most
    load-bearing document found.</li>
    <li><strong>Matteo's other son is traceable too.</strong> Giuseppe Falco married Antonia di Guida,
    and their household can be followed for three more decades — including a son named
    <strong>Matteo after his grandfather</strong>, who died in 1809.</li>
    <li><strong>The Crisci marry in three times.</strong> Matteo married a Crisci in the 1760s; his
    grandson Vincenzo married Andreana Crisci two generations later; and Pasquale's daughter Anna
    married an Arcangelo Crisci and died at 22 in 1820.</li>
    <li><strong>One marriage needed the Pope.</strong> Crescenzo Falco, son of Giuseppe and Antonia di
    Guida, married Angela Maria Morgillo only <em>medio dispensatione obtenta a Sanctissimo Domino
    Papa nostro Gregorio super quarto gradu consanguinitatis</em> — by dispensation from Gregory XVI
    for kinship in the fourth degree. The couple were third cousins, and the parish knew it.</li>
    <li><strong>And one Falco married a Falco.</strong> Francesco Falco and Raffaela Falco buried their
    son Clemente, aged five, in August 1855.</li>
    <li><strong>Children die.</strong> Maria Angela, baptised February 1802, was buried in March 1805
    <em>aetatis suae annorum 3 circiter</em>. Giovanna was buried the same year. Giuseppe, son of
    Crescenzo, lived a few days. The name Maria was given twice, in 1789 and again in 1790.</li>
    <li><strong>The same women recur.</strong> <em>Cecilia Martone</em> appears as midwife or godmother
    across some twenty years of these registers; <em>Gelsumina Ruggiero</em> stands godmother to
    Vincenzo's children. A parish is a small number of people doing the same offices for one another.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it stops</h2>
  <p>Above Matteo Falco the trail ends for now. His marriage to Francesca Crisci — which would name his
  own father — should fall in the Arienzo marriage register between about 1760 and 1766. It has been
  searched for and not found: it is either not in the transcribed pages, or not filmed. His baptism
  would fall in the gap the baptism register leaves between <strong>1738 and 1761</strong>.</p>
</Base>
